WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.319 --> 00:00:06.650
A corrections facility in Lipetsk, a few hundred
kilometers south-east of Moscow.

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:10.849
Like all Russian penitentiaries, this one
is over-crowded.

00:00:10.849 --> 00:00:15.269
Human rights activists complain that way too
many people are being put behind bars, that

00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:20.949
it cultivates a so-called "convict-culture"
and helps make Russian society violent.

00:00:20.949 --> 00:00:24.570
It's also reminiscent of the Gulag regime
of Stalin's reign.

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:30.039
Olga Romanova of the Moscow foundation "Russia
Behind Bars" says many inmates are the victims

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:34.280
of a justice system rife with corruption and
lack of professionalism.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.309
Her organization tries to help the alleged
victims of questionable verdicts.

00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:45.570
"When you think of 'Russia Behind Bars,' we
have this saying: One third of inmates are

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.230
serving time for the wrong reason, one third
for no reason at all, and only one third for

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:57.010
crimes they really did commit."

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.850
These four Russians were also sent to prison.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:05.030
They were taken to court for various reasons
and at various times – and all were sentenced.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.540
Now they're talking openly about their time
behind bars.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:13.610
"First of all they use brutal methods to break
you.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.680
Your psyche and your body are abused and humiliated."

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.080
"You can only have one visit every four to
five months.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.780
I didn't know how I was supposed to survive
being separated from my loved ones – without

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.320
being able to even hug them."

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.030
"The worst were those who died in prison.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:35.229
While I was there, 18 passed away – about
one every two months.

00:01:35.229 --> 00:01:38.840
All because of faults in the system."

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:45.250
"Russian judicial organizations and courts
have simply turned into pencil-pushers.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:50.100
Justice is the last thing that interests them."

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.399
Critics complain that the state itself is
actually creating criminals – just to make

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:55.399
a buck.

00:01:55.399 --> 00:02:00.320
"If a police station has arrested a hundred
people in January, you can bet that it won't

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.399
have any less in February.

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:04.380
That wouldn't correspond to the national plan.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.670
110 would be better, without a doubt.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.830
Prison works on per-person funding.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:13.170
The more inmates, the more money the institution
gets.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:18.080
And consequently the more money for judges,
district attorneys and investigators.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.900
So they claim that there are enemies and criminals
everywhere.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.380
But it's really just about money."

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:28.480
Even President Vladimir Putin has said on
several occasions that the country's justice

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.680
system needs to be reformed.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.040
But so far, nothing has really changed in
Russia's prisons.

